# Cursor Documentation Rules (C++ Source + Tools)

## OVERVIEW
All source files, methods, and tools MUST be thoroughly documented.
Documentation is NOT optional. Any code change without documentation updates is invalid.

---

# SOURCE CODE DOCUMENTATION

## File Location
/docs/source_doc.md

---

## When to Update
- After ANY code change
- After adding/removing methods
- After refactoring
- No task is complete until documentation is updated

---

## File-Level Documentation (Required)

FILE: <filename>

PURPOSE:
What this file is responsible for

DEPENDENCIES:
Headers, classes, or systems it relies on

KEY COMPONENTS:
List of major classes/functions

NOTES:
Important design decisions or constraints

---

## Class Documentation (Required if applicable)

CLASS: <name>

PURPOSE:
What the class represents

MEMBERS:
- variable: type — purpose

METHODS:
List of key methods

INVARIANTS:
Conditions that must always be true

---

## Method / Function Documentation (Required)

FUNCTION: <name>

SIGNATURE:
<full function signature>

DESCRIPTION:
What it does (clear and direct)

PARAMETERS:
- name: type — purpose

RETURNS:
type — what is returned

SIDE EFFECTS:
State changes, I/O, mutations

ERROR HANDLING:
What happens on failure / edge cases

DEPENDENCIES:
Other functions/classes used

---

## Writing Rules
- Be precise and technical
- No vague phrases (e.g., “handles stuff”)
- Explain intent, not obvious syntax
- Use exact names from code
- Keep formatting consistent
- One section per file, cleanly grouped

---

# TOOLS DOCUMENTATION

## File Location
/docs/tools_doc.md

---

## What Counts as a Tool
- Build scripts
- CLI utilities
- Python/bash scripts
- Automation tools
- Internal helper programs

---

## When to Update
- After any logic change
- After changing CLI arguments
- After changing outputs
- No tool change is complete without documentation

---

## Tool-Level Documentation (Required)

TOOL: <name>

PURPOSE:
What the tool does

USAGE:
Command or how to run it

INPUT:
Required inputs (args, files, flags)

OUTPUT:
What it produces

DEPENDENCIES:
External libraries, binaries, environment

SIDE EFFECTS:
Files modified, system changes

ERROR HANDLING:
Failure behavior

NOTES:
Constraints, limitations

---

## Tool Internal Functions (If Needed)

FUNCTION: <name>

DESCRIPTION:
What it does

INPUT:
Parameters

OUTPUT:
Return values

SIDE EFFECTS:
Any state or file changes

---

# GLOBAL ENFORCEMENT RULES (CRITICAL)

- Documentation is mandatory
- No undocumented code changes
- No missing sections
- No vague descriptions
- No combining multiple responsibilities in one entry

# INDENTATION RULES (CRITICAL)

## General Format (source_doc / tools_doc)

- **Anchor lines** (`FILE: <path>` in `docs/source_doc.md`, `TOOL: <path>` in `docs/tools_doc.md`) are **left-aligned at column 0** (no leading spaces).
- **Everything that belongs to that entry** (from the first blank line after the anchor until the line before the next anchor) is indented with **one extra 4-space block** from column 0. Concretely: each such line starts with exactly **4 spaces** more than it would in a flat layout, so readers see the file/tool name at the margin and the whole entry nested beneath it.
- **Within** that nested block, keep **relative** depth: section labels (`PURPOSE:`, `FUNCTION:`, `DESCRIPTION:`, etc.) start at **4 spaces** from column 0. A normal value line starts at **8 spaces**. Bullets under `DEPENDENCIES:`, `KEY COMPONENTS:`, `MEMBERS:`, `INPUT:`, and similar “dense list” fields use **12 spaces** (legacy double indent inside the entry). Hyphen continuation lines under `PARAMETERS:` use **8 spaces** when they are written as wrapped parameter rows (same as other 8-space body lines in this repository).
- **No tabs** — spaces only.

---

## Correct Structure Example (inside one `FILE:` block)

FILE: example.cpp

    PURPOSE:
        One-line summary of the file.

    DEPENDENCIES:
            - other_header.h

    FUNCTION: calculateDamage

    SIGNATURE:
        int calculateDamage(const Pokemon& attacker, const Pokemon& defender)

    DESCRIPTION:
        Calculates damage based on stats, type, and modifiers

    PARAMETERS:
        - attacker: Pokemon — attacking unit
        - defender: Pokemon — defending unit

    RETURNS:
        int — final damage value

    SIDE EFFECTS:
        None

    ERROR HANDLING:
        Returns 0 if invalid stats detected

    DEPENDENCIES:
            - TypeChart
            - RNG

---

## Rules

- NEVER put values on the same line as the label
  Wrong:
  SIGNATURE: int main()
  Right:
  SIGNATURE:
        int main()

- NEVER dedent content inside a `FILE:` / `TOOL:` block to column 0 except the anchor line itself
- Multi-line values keep the same indentation as the first value line

---

## Multi-line Value Example

FILE: runtime.cpp

    DESCRIPTION:
        Creates the game runtime object and runs the main loop.
        Handles initialization and shutdown of core systems.

---

## Empty Fields

If a section has no content:

FILE: stub.cpp

    PARAMETERS:
        None

---

## Consistency Enforcement

- Indentation must be identical across ALL files
- No tabs allowed — spaces only
- Misaligned documentation is considered invalid
- Cursor MUST fix indentation before completing a task

Cursor MUST:
- Update /docs/source_doc.md for ANY source change
- Update /docs/tools_doc.md for ANY tool change

If documentation is outdated or missing:
STOP and fix documentation before continuing
